\section*{Gigli distributional sectional-curvature characterization}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Distributional characterization of Alexandrov curvature}: The paper claims equivalence between lower Alexandrov curvature and the corresponding noncollapsed RCD bound together with Gigli’s distributional sectional inequality. It uses the original global test classes for every integer dimension at least two.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Distributional characterization of Alexandrov curvature}: RCD calculus supplies the starting transport and differential structure. Additional index, frame, and distance-comparison arguments turn the distributional sectional condition into geometric triangle comparison. The companion reweighting theorem propagates weak Hessian bounds to prescribed geodesics, enabling the curvature implication without replacing the original test classes by stronger ones.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Distributional characterization of Alexandrov curvature}: The paper claims equivalence between lower Alexandrov curvature and the corresponding noncollapsed RCD bound together with Gigli’s distributional sectional inequality. It uses the original global test classes for every integer dimension at least two. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 356 describes Lean coverage for the weak-Hessian inequality on every geodesic; the full sectional-curvature characterization is separate.

\textit{Distributional characterization of Alexandrov curvature}: No manuscript-specific Comparator is listed in the pinned inventory.

The documented selection does not cover the entire manuscript assertion relevant here.

